## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about data-parallel bit tricks and boundary-safe shifts without branches.

## Key Concepts

- Byte-wise (SWAR) operations across 128-bit lanes
- Constructing per-byte masks to constrain operations to byte boundaries
- Handling n in 0..7 without loops or conditionals

## Code Example

```cpp
// outline of approach (conceptual; leverages 128-bit value split into bytes)
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 perByteRotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
    uint128 M = { 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL };
    uint128 left  = { .hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (8 - n)), .lo = x.lo << n };
    uint128 right = { .hi = (x.hi >> (8 - n)), .lo = (x.lo >> (8 - n)) };
    return { .hi = (left.hi & M.hi) | (right.hi & ~M.hi),
             .lo = (left.lo & M.lo) | (right.lo & ~M.lo) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 32-bit lanes or to 256-bit types?
- What are the performance implications on modern compilers and ISAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?

Why this is asked
- Tests ability to implement 128-bit rotation without branching, using a safe 128-bit intermediate. Covers edge cases n=0 and n=64 cleanly.

Key concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic, circular shift, branch-free implementation, handling wrap-around.

Code example
```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  __uint128_t full = ((__uint128_t)x.hi << 64) | x.lo;
  unsigned k = n & 127;
  __uint128_t r = (full << k) | (full >> (128 - k));
  uint128 out; out.hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64); out.lo = (uint64_t)r; return out;
}
```

Follow-up questions
- How would you implement rotateRight128 without 128-bit type?
- Discuss performance when 128-bit shifts are not optimized by the compiler.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 128-bit data manipulation, endianness, and composing simple bit tricks to achieve a larger transformation without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value composed of two 64-bit halves
- Byte reversal within 64-bit words (endian swap)
- Combining results to form final 128-bit reversal
- No loops; relies on fixed-mask shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}

void endianSwap128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = reverse64(lo);
  *out_lo = reverse64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify correctness for non-trivial patterns quickly?
- How does this approach scale if you extend to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to derive a compact, branch-free bit-twisting routine for combinatorial generation.

## Key Concepts

- Next permutation with fixed popcount via snoob
- Branchless 64-bit arithmetic and overflow behavior
- Edge-case handling in tests

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextWithSameBitCount(uint64_t x){
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
  ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
  return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for arbitrary precision (128-bit) using two limbs?
- How would you detect when no next permutation exists efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits to form Morton codes is common in spatial indexing and GPU data layouts. The solution enforces branchless bit spreading using fixed masks, ensuring deterministic performance.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order (Z-order) bit interleaving
- Fixed-mask bit spreading (part1by1)
- Branchless implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint32_t n) {
  uint64_t v = n;
  v = (v | (v << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  v = (v | (v << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  v = (v | (v << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return v;
}

uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs producing a 128-bit result?
- How does this interact with SIMD paths for batched data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of bit-twiddling tricks for 128-bit values without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR/popcount for 64-bit chunks
- Safe 128-bit aggregation without branches

## Code Example

```c
uint32_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  auto count64 = [](uint64_t v){
    v = v - ((v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v>>8);
    v = v + (v>>16);
    v = v + (v>>32);
    return (uint64_t)(v & 0x7F);
  };
  uint64_t c1 = count64(hi);
  uint64_t c2 = count64(lo);
  return (uint32_t)(c1 + c2);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit value?
- What are the risks of using this in performance-critical hot paths compared to builtin intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate a 128-bit value by composing 64-bit operations, enforcing a non-loop, fixed-sequence approach for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and byte-level reversal
- Bit-twiddling for 64-bit reversal
- Combining two 64-bit halves into a 128-bit result

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t v) {
  v = ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((v >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  v = ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((v >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  v = (v << 32) | (v >> 32);
  return v;
}
uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if only a single 128-bit type isn’t available?
- How would you validate correctness with representative test patterns (all zeros, all ones, alternating bits)?

